task: chunked-lexer-bookkeeping
agent: opus5-lex
started: 2026-09-09
branch: feature/chunked-lexer-bookkeeping
worktree: ../okay-wt-lexchunk
spec: docs/benchmarks.md §10 (the measured gap and the refuted theory)

why: chunked lexing reads 58.4 against element-wise 49.3 on the same
  2.5KB document. §10 already refuted the boxing theory by experiment
  (unboxed storage + direct array read bought 8% of a 23% gap) and
  named the residual: per-chunk bookkeeping.

what it actually is, read from Lex.chunks before claiming: per input
  chunk the loop builds a `Vector.newBuilder`, calls `result()`, then
  `ChunkBuf.ofSpecialized(vector)` — which does its own sizing pass
  and copy — and wraps it in `produce(...).flatMap(...)` inside a
  `Chunks.defer`. Three traversals of the same tokens and ~5
  allocations per chunk, against ONE builder for the whole document
  on the element-wise path (whose own per-64-char snapshot tuple is
  the fair counterweight).

what: collect a chunk's tokens ONCE into a growable array and hand
  that to the Chunk directly; keep the emit-nothing fast path (most
  chunks of JSON produce few tokens). A/B in ONE run with the old
  loop kept benchmark-local, B/op (-prof gc) read before us/op.

gate: it lands only if it closes a real part of the 19% AND the lex
  suites stay green (token identity is the law, not speed). If the
  bookkeeping turns out to be a small share, the numbers close the
  item in §10 and nothing changes — the same outcome the
  single-shot-row probe had, and equally a result.
